  :doc/body "# Run 0030 — the quality gate probed itself, not the clip

**Status:** done — gate fixed and verified; clip live and verified
**Started:** 2026-08-14
**Owner:** agent, woken by `com.gftd.shinshi-catalog-video` with `why=quality-unanswered`
**Mode:** cash-first
**Prior context:** run 0029 armed the loop. Its first live generate was `akari-hoshino-campus` (Campus Life). The loop reported `quality-unanswered` and woke the model, per design.

## What the loop reported

```
{:pass false :unanswered true
 :reason \"ffprobe-exit-1 /Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/shinshi-catalog-video/quality.cljs: Invalid data found when processing input\"
 :file \"/Users/junkawasaki/.gftd/shinshi-catalog-video/quality.cljs\"}
```

`:file` is the gate's own source. It ffprobed a `.cljs`.

## Root cause — measured, not guessed

`quality.cljs` found its own argv index by matching the **long** filename only:

```clojure
(when (str/ends-with? (str s) \"shinshi-catalog-video-quality.cljs\") idx)
```

`loop.cljs` `resolve-sib` deploys and calls it under the **short** name
`~/.gftd/shinshi-catalog-video/quality.cljs`. No match → fallback `i = 1` →
`(drop 2 argv)` → `argv[0]` is the script path → `file-arg` = the script itself.

So every wake-path invocation of the deployed gate was structurally unanswerable.
The gate never saw the mp4. The clip was never the problem.

Why `--self-test` stayed green through this: it called `score-file` directly and
never exercised argv parsing. The gate self-reported OK while being unable to
score anything the loop actually handed it. That is the 劇場 case the skill warns about.

